== Computational and multi-agent relevance ==
In [[Related to::Multi-agent system|multi-agent systems]], a collective-action problem can be represented by a set of actors, available contributions, individual costs and benefits, information each actor can observe, and a rule mapping contributions to shared outcomes. The substantive computational question is how local decisions and communication affect a system-level result; simply counting people who cooperate is not enough to specify the mechanism.

For example, contributors to an open knowledge service may collectively benefit from maintenance while each would prefer someone else to bear some cost. A model can compare voluntary contributions, membership rules, shared funding, reputation signals or repeated interaction under explicit assumptions. Changing the incentives may change equilibrium behavior; neither self-interest nor cooperation alone guarantees an outcome.

This is an '''application of game theory and distributed coordination''', not a claim that every institution is a computer. A modeled equilibrium, a participant's observed behavior and a proposed governance rule must remain distinct. See [[Mechanism design]], [[Incentive]] and [[Commons]].

